<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <Platforms>AnyCPU</Platforms>
    <Product>SRT Overlay Direct3D 12</Product>
    <Description>Direct3D 12 half of the injected overlay shim: vtable resolution and the Present hook.</Description>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No Vortice.Windows, and that is a departure from what section 11 settled - so it is written down
    rather than left to be discovered.

    Vortice is the right answer on the plugin's side of the boundary, where an author renders in their
    own process and ergonomics are worth a dependency. It is the wrong answer here: this assembly is
    linked into the one binary that gets injected into other people's games, where the dependency
    closure is a reputation question, and section 11 already commits the shim to being trivially
    explicable to anyone who looks at it.

    The saving is real rather than theoretical. What the shim needs from D3D12 is four exported
    functions and a handful of vtable slots called by index - and it needs the raw vtable pointers
    regardless, which is exactly what a typed wrapper hides. Hand-rolled interop is a few hundred
    lines here versus a library that would have to be trimmed, AOT-verified and vouched for.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SRTOverlay.Core\SRTOverlay.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SRTOverlay.Protocol\SRTOverlay.Protocol.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
